{
  "_comment": "Copy to <main checkout>/.agents/team/escalation.local.json (gitignored). send and wait are each one simple command: first word bash or sh (found on PATH, no absolute path), then the adapter (no option word -… or +… before it) and its fixed words, ending with the six positionals \"$1\" \"$2\" \"$3\" \"$4\" \"$5\" \"$6\" ($1=payload file, $2=team, $3=role, $4=question id, $5=thread esc-<run>-<qid>, $6=seq); no ; & | < > ` ' $( or other $. team-escalate.sh parses both commands against this pattern without running them, before any adapter starts; one outside it is refused (NO-REPLY invalid config: adapter does not forward thread/seq). Why: the adapter's dedup key is <thread>-<seq>, unique per run; without $5/$6 it falls back to a per-qid key, a qid reused in a later run collides and the message is silently dropped. A send counts only when the adapter exits 0 and its last stdout line is SENT id=<n>; anything else is a failed attempt. rc 0 without it is retried once, then NO-REPLY invalid adapter (no SENT receipt); with peek configured the thread is checked first. wait prints one Advisor reply: optional 'mail-id: <id>', 'status: answer|pending-operator|withdrawn', optional 'author: <names>', 'decided-by: analysts|operator', 'operator-ref: <note> sha256:<prefix>', 'eta: <text>', a blank line, then the body; it stages the reply to <dir of $1>/<qid>.reply-<mail-id>.md before acking. timeout_min bounds each send and the wait for a first reply (1-240). Missing file, error or timeout → NO-REPLY: the supervisor re-pings Advisor and tells the Operator in one line — it never forwards the question. opinion (optional): the same template rules as send/wait; it prints Reviewer's newest message on the thread (a 'mail-id: <id>' line + the body) or nothing, read-only, never acking; an absent key turns the Advisor no-reply fallback off (.agents/reference/escalation-fallback.md). peek (optional, the same template rules): one poll of the inboxes wait reads; it prints a Advisor reply exactly as wait does (staged and acked first) or exactly one line EMPTY, exit 0; an error exits non-zero. The fallback is written only after an EMPTY; an absent key turns it off as well.",
  "send": "bash /path/to/escalate.sh send \"$1\" \"$2\" \"$3\" \"$4\" \"$5\" \"$6\"",
  "wait": "bash /path/to/escalate.sh wait \"$1\" \"$2\" \"$3\" \"$4\" \"$5\" \"$6\"",
  "opinion": "bash /path/to/escalate.sh opinion \"$1\" \"$2\" \"$3\" \"$4\" \"$5\" \"$6\"",
  "peek": "bash /path/to/escalate.sh peek \"$1\" \"$2\" \"$3\" \"$4\" \"$5\" \"$6\"",
  "timeout_min": 30
}
